Z||000001||Raydar||07-11-2001||08:15 PM||||The numbers seem to indicate that it's running rich. (Burning too much gas, and/or not burning it completely.) <BR>Spark plugs, wires, or distributor cap can cause that if they are in bad shape. <BR>A clogged air filter can also cause high CO readings. <P>Was the car completely warmed up? If not it'll never pass. Leave your AC turned off. <P>You also might try the oxygen sensor. They're a pain in the behind to replace, though.  <P>If you get a code that says "lean exhaust", it probably is the O2 sensor. I forget what the code is, though. <P>Maybe someone else?<P>Good luck.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||07-11-2001||08:32 PM||||After the 2<sup>nd</sup> E check failure you should be allowed to spend a few bucks and get an automatic "pass".  Pretty sure that's the way it is in Butler Co.  Or, you can register your car in one of the less communist counties that don't require an E check.<P>------------------<BR>'Consciousness:  That annoying time between naps.'<P>MM||||reg||1
